WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.200
Welcome from a very sunny and beautiful Aruba.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:05.670
No, I'm kidding.

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:06.670
It's a pandemic.

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:10.910
I don't leave Massachusetts right now, but
there is a lot to talk about.

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:17.170
Last week, I did a story called We Owe the
World an Apology and an Explanation.

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:22.760
And that story was really focused on having
to sort of make good with the world for the

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:28.660
fact that more than 60 million Americans voted
for Donald Trump in 2016.

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:36.480
And in so doing, unleashed absolutely unconscionable
destruction upon the world.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:37.739
That was last week.

00:00:37.739 --> 00:00:43.010
And the focus was mostly the micro while touching
on some of the big areas where the United

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:47.030
States has been really a drag on the world
under Donald Trump.

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:48.620
So we talked about climate change.

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:50.870
Of course, we talked about naturally.

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:55.280
How could I not mention our disastrous response
to coronavirus?

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:59.760
But there's an entire sort of other story
to tell about the United States under Trump,

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:05.670
which is less about Trump per say and more
about the way in which our status quo has

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:11.380
been exposed as dangerously fragile in so
many ways, which is something that even people

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:18.580
who are not necessarily Trump ists have been
sometimes hesitant to really consider as seriously

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:19.830
as as we should.

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:23.430
And of course, the virus itself has exposed
a lot of fragility.

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:27.750
A lot of jobs that seemed rock solid were
not really Robbs rock solid.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:31.860
Entire industries, quite frankly, were not
as solid as we thought.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:36.400
Our supply chains, we figured out very quickly,
are very fragile, the medical system.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.430
Of course, that's useful to think about.

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:45.060
But there is a way more sort of pernicious
fragility that has been exposed, including,

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:49.720
for example, our alliances with other countries
around the world.

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:53.170
This is not corona virus specifically now.

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:59.280
This is what I'd call a sort of trump fragility
allies that we felt were rock solid.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:08.789
Our primary important allies were very quickly,
as soon as the United States was challenged

00:02:08.789 --> 00:02:12.900
and was no longer a reliable, predictable
partner.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:17.000
And instead, we became this sort of erratic,
counterproductive fiasco.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:18.640
Many of our allies abandoned us.

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:21.800
Now, they didn't do it in a way where they
would now be considered adversaries, certainly

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:24.810
not, but to different degrees in in their
own ways.

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:31.180
We saw what we thought were very solid alliances
be exposed as extremely fragile very quickly.

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:37.250
Germany, France and Canada, for example, realized
we can't deal with Trump like an adult.

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:42.790
We're actually better off without the American
president in the room at all when we're having

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:44.870
certain conversations.

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:48.209
That's embarrassing and that's bad for us
long term.

00:02:48.209 --> 00:02:53.930
Now, I don't necessarily want to go directly
into a sort of geopolitics game theory type

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:58.290
of analysis, but it is kind of hard to avoid
when you look at what's going on around the

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:04.380
world, because when you look at the superpowers
of the world, U.S., Russia and China, and

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:08.140
you think about the last four years, it's
not looking particularly good for the United

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:09.140
States.

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:15.849
This may not be your top priority, but if
you concede that the most likely superpower

00:03:15.849 --> 00:03:23.300
in the world is likely to be either the U.S.,
China or Russia for some time, a United States

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:27.640
that's increasingly isolated and losing allies
is bad.

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:33.140
It's bad for Americans as China gains leverage
in the increasingly failed trade war that

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:34.870
Donald Trump has been waging against them.

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:41.470
As Russia continues to benefit from the destabilization
and the lack of credibility of American democracy

00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:45.490
partially at their own doing, of course, we
suffer.

00:03:45.490 --> 00:03:50.190
There's a very good article in Rolling Stone
from this weekend by Wade Davis called The

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:57.659
Unraveling of America, and it focuses on this
debasement of America in the context of Corona

00:03:57.659 --> 00:03:58.659
virus.

00:03:58.659 --> 00:04:03.430
The article is great, but almost every argument
that Wade Davis makes and he makes the arguments

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:08.580
well with respect to Corona virus, we could
actually apply in the United States more broadly.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:10.319
I'll give you an example.

00:04:10.319 --> 00:04:13.440
Davis says in this article, which you should
read in its entirety.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:19.380
The American handling of Corona virus has
called into question the idea of American

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:20.720
exceptionalism.

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:22.079
I would go further.

00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:29.910
Our failed response to Corona virus has confirmed
that the idea of American exceptionalism was

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:34.300
but a shiny Band-Aid covering up serious injuries.

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:41.410
It exposed many of the cultural failings that
Corona virus has made clear.

00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:47.330
The lack of our ability to really come together
as a country to do what just needs to be done,

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:50.229
even if it's difficult for the betterment
of humanity.

00:04:50.229 --> 00:04:53.850
The financial fragility has been exposed.

00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:59.780
Wade Daviss article reminds us that almost
every empire has fallen the ends of.

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:04.270
Empires are rarely anticipated and they often
take a while.

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:07.430
But you don't notice that the failing empire
is failing.

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:14.000
Until you look back later, it's the United
States this time that needs the world's help,

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:18.289
as just about every other country has better
dealt with coronavirus than we have.

00:05:18.289 --> 00:05:20.570
So what is it that hurt us?

00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:25.430
Yeah, you know, of course, Trump's response
was a disaster, but there's more.

00:05:25.430 --> 00:05:27.280
There's really cultural problems.

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:30.520
Is it too much individualism in the United
States?

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:33.790
Is it a lack of education or of good enough
education?

00:05:33.790 --> 00:05:35.789
Delusions of grandeur.

00:05:35.789 --> 00:05:36.789
All of the above.

00:05:36.789 --> 00:05:39.289
Yeah, probably some component of all of those.

00:05:39.289 --> 00:05:42.630
So I encourage you to check out the piece
in Rolling Stone.

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:48.039
And if we step back for a moment, from the
sort of day to day of the Trump administration,

00:05:48.039 --> 00:05:54.860
what has become clear is that beyond Donald
Trump himself, this type of way of running

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:59.660
the country is not going to allow us to solve
the big problems that we're facing.

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:03.730
So in no particular order, step back from
Corona virus a little bit.

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:07.699
If you think about climate change, if you
think about getting everybody health care,

00:06:07.699 --> 00:06:11.680
if you think about ending optional poverty
and hunger, which are optional right now,

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:14.310
I mean, it's just we've just not decided to
fix them.

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:20.930
But hunger and homelessness are optional at
this point in time, creating government that

00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:22.759
is not crony a stick and corrupt.

00:06:22.759 --> 00:06:24.430
Big, big things.

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:30.590
None of those problems are going to even be
seriously explored from the standpoint of

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:34.240
solutions under an administration like the
one we have.

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:40.490
It's not just the day to day pedestrian xenophobia
that's an afterthought, but it's the pseudo

00:06:40.490 --> 00:06:46.990
intellectual ism, the anti intellectual ism,
the hyper nationalism, the intellectual isolationism.

00:06:46.990 --> 00:06:51.440
That's when you look at how every other country
realizes this is a time to trust science and

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:55.550
doctors and we are isolated intellectually
or a..

00:06:55.550 --> 00:06:57.520
Intellectually, where you have people thing.

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:01.580
Foushee and Bill Gates are the enemies here.

00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:04.039
That's not suited for the 21st century and
beyond.

00:07:04.039 --> 00:07:07.610
And it's hurting us now visibly with corona
virus.

00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:13.319
But it's more about the list of problems we
won't be able to solve if this is how the

00:07:13.319 --> 00:07:15.539
United States operates more broadly.

00:07:15.539 --> 00:07:20.780
Now, I know many of you will say, well, OK,
63 million people voted for this administration,

00:07:20.780 --> 00:07:27.940
65, 66 voted against it for Hillary Clinton,
that this is not about the electoral politics.

00:07:27.940 --> 00:07:32.900
This is about a realization of what is the
path forward for humans with something that

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:34.200
is very antiquated.

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:39.310
And by the way, when I say antiquated, I'm
more than willing to entertain the argument

00:07:39.310 --> 00:07:43.419
that in times past this type of mentality
made more sense.

00:07:43.419 --> 00:07:48.780
I'm sensitive to the idea that an early humanity,
many of the traits that now are a problem

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:56.460
were adaptive from an evolutionary standpoint,
favoring these ideas of, you know, hypermasculine,

00:07:56.460 --> 00:08:02.580
alpha, male, physical violence, individualism
for yourself or your small group isolationism.

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:06.960
There was a time in human history where that
made sense, and that was a totally reasonable

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:08.530
thing to think about.

00:08:08.530 --> 00:08:09.800
But it doesn't make sense now.

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:14.120
And the people sticking to those ideas, Díaz,
are quite literally stuck in the past when

00:08:14.120 --> 00:08:16.389
we say conservatism.

00:08:16.389 --> 00:08:21.370
What we're talking about is trying to conserve
a way of life from the past merely because

00:08:21.370 --> 00:08:22.780
it's what we used to do.

00:08:22.780 --> 00:08:25.120
Even if it doesn't make any sense now or in
the future.

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:27.169
And that's exactly where we are today.

00:08:27.169 --> 00:08:30.700
A lot to think about when you bring it back
to well.

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:33.970
We're less than three months from an important
election.

00:08:33.970 --> 00:08:38.180
And today I'm going to be outlining some of
the big differences between the options that

00:08:38.180 --> 00:08:40.350
we have in November, differences that maybe
are not the ones that are getting the most

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:40.380
attention.

